00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.400
Tables are useful when they hold the state
of your work, but sometimes a column

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:09.280
shouldn't be typed in by hand. It should
be produced by workflow. Workflow columns

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:14.520
let a table run your logic row by row,
write the result back into the right cell,

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:20.720
and then keep going as new rows become
ready. So a table stops being just the

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:25.520
place to store results and becomes a
pipeline that fills itself. Let me show

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:26.520
you how it works.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:33.060
Alongside your workflows, a workspace can
hold tables. This one tracks AI startup

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:38.660
leads, and so far, only the company column
has been typed in. A workflow column is

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:42.780
different. Instead of typing its values
in, you attach one of your workflows, and

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:48.440
that workflow fills the column for you.
Everything that defines it lives in one

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:52.240
panel, starting with which of your
workflows runs. Here, it's a small

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:57.700
workflow that finds a company's website.
Its inputs are bound to columns. On every

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:03.180
row, the workflow will read that row's
company value as its input. And its output

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:08.040
is bound to the column it fills. So each
run writes one cell right beside its

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:13.200
source. You can run a single row to watch
it work. The cell reports the run as it

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:19.100
moves, queued first, then running. Under
that badge is a real workflow run. The

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:23.140
row's company value lands at the start
block, and the agent picks it up as its

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:28.740
input. When the run finishes, the output
is written back into the row. One row in,

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:34.240
one run, one value back. You can attach
more than one group to the same table.

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:38.320
This one scores each lead, and it reads
the domain column that the first group

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:43.220
fills. That dependency is called run
after. The row that already has a domain

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:49.200
queues up immediately, and the empty rows
